WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
Think of a tree.

2
00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:04.030
Not long ago it was a seed.

3
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.270
One day it will fall and rot away.

4
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.620
Now look further: you,
this planet, the stars.

5
00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.110
Each came into being,
and each will pass away.

6
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.250
None of them has to exist.

7
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.870
Each could simply never have been here.

8
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.280
So if everything that exists
could have not existed,

9
00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.740
why is there anything at all?

10
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:29.970
In this mission, you'll start, as
every cosmological argument does,

11
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.950
from the world we can see.

12
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.420
Then you'll follow a
thirteenth-century friar,

13
00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.470
Thomas Aquinas, one step at a time.

14
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.920
Your mission: work out what Aquinas thinks
the existence of anything at all requires.
